          ],
          "bullets": [
            "TechCrunch reports OpenAI said the watermark will roll out \"over the coming weeks to eligible ChatGPT and Codex users on all plans, but only in the EU\", while API developers anywhere can turn it on for select models from 5 October, off by default. The method, textGrain, shapes the model's word choices so a detector can recover a statistical pattern from the text alone.",
            "The Next Web reports OpenAI's own limits: at a target false positive rate of 1%, the detector found the watermark in about 80% of 200-token passages and about 95% of 400-token passages; replacing 10% of words with synonyms cut detection from about 92% to 66%, and replacing a quarter of the words cut it to 17%. Detection was \"substantially lower for maths, where word choice is less flexible\".",
            "OpenAI wrote that \"these limitations contribute to our decision to provide initial detector access only to approved researchers and expert organizations\", and cautioned that a missing watermark \"does not prove human authorship\". The Next Web reports Article 50 of the AI Act gives providers already on the market until 2 December to comply. OpenAI's own post page returned HTTP 403 and could not be read directly."
