      "type": "item",
      "section": "Military, defense & geopolitics",
      "headline": "Pentagon tells BBC it \"has ceased the use of Anthropic products\" after a six-month phaseout",
      "lines": [
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "A Defense Department official said on Monday that the Pentagon has ceased the use of Anthropic products. Defence Secretary Pete Hegseth labelled Anthropic a supply chain risk in February and said the Pentagon would stop using it by late August. The BBC reports it is not clear why there has been a delay."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "Had it actually stopped before now?"
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "Apparently not. The BBC reports that former defence officials and contractors who worked with the Pentagon on AI said that as recently as last week Claude was still being used in research, analysis and intelligence gathering, and in military operations against Iran."
        },
        {
          "host": "B",
          "text": "Where does that leave the dispute?"
        },
        {
          "host": "A",
          "text": "Anthropic called the designation unprecedented and unlawful and is suing to overturn it. The BBC reports the department has since signed contracts with Google, xAI and OpenAI. This is one outlet's reporting, it's an update to a story we've followed, and the BBC's own page couldn't be opened, so we read it from a syndicated copy."
        }
      ]
